for was struck, by no means the worst man among the Myrmidons,
the son of great-hearted Agacles, heavenly Epeigeus,
who in Budeion, well-inhabited, ruled
formerly;

And for Patroclus grief came about his companion, who had perished,
and he charged through the front-fighters, like to a hawk
swift, which puts to flight jackdaws and starlings;

for forward he bent down, and that, from behind, spear, long,
in the ground was fixed, and upon it the butt-end was shaken
of the spear;

“Aeneas, hard it is for you, though mighty being,
of all men to quench the might, whoever against you
may come, defending himself;

And they ever around the corpse kept thronging, as when flies
in a steading buzz about the pails brimming with milk
in the season of spring, when the milk wets the vessels;

Then not even the mighty Lycians stayed, but were put to flight,
all, when they saw their king, harmed in heart,
lying in a gathering of the dead;

and him then
far away bearing, wash in the streams of a river,
and anoint with ambrosia, and about him deathless garments put;
